---
import Doc from "../../layouts/Doc.astro";
import MethodologyNav from "../../components/MethodologyNav.astro";
import MethodFacts from "../../components/MethodFacts.astro";
import { M, axisByKey, percent, stackingPercents, stackingTailPercent } from "../../lib/methodology";

const axis = axisByKey("dependency_health");
const cap = (s: string) => s[0].toUpperCase() + s.slice(1);
const rows = M.dependency_deductions;
const cvss = (i: number) =>
  i === 0
    ? `${rows[0].cvss_min} and above`
    : rows[i].cvss_min === "0"
      ? `below ${rows[i - 1].cvss_min}`
      : `${rows[i].cvss_min} to below ${rows[i - 1].cvss_min}`;
---

<Doc
  title="Dependency Health — mcpwatchman methodology"
  description="How mcpwatchman's Dependency Health axis scores MCP servers: known vulnerabilities in pinned dependencies via osv-scanner, deducted by CVSS band and direct or transitive."
  canonical="https://mcpwatchman.com/methodology/dependency-health/"
  heading="Dependency Health"
  crumb={{ href: "/methodology/", label: "Methodology" }}
>
  <Fragment slot="lede">
    <p>
      Exposure to known vulnerabilities in the dependencies a server pins —
      the classic supply-chain risk. Provisional weight{" "}
      {percent(axis.weight)}%.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
  <MethodFacts slot="facts" />

  <MethodologyNav />

  <section class="doc-section" aria-labelledby="reads-heading">
    <h2 id="reads-heading">What is read</h2>
    <p>
      osv-scanner runs against the lockfiles in the server's published source
      and matches every pinned version against the OSV vulnerability
      database.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Only what a publisher pinned is attributed to them.</strong> A
      lockfile pins; so does a requirements file, but only for the
      requirements it pins exactly with <code>==</code>. A version the scanner
      resolved on its own is never attributed. An ecosystem declared only by
      unpinned requirements was not measured, so the axis covers only the
      ecosystems a lockfile did, and <code>assessed_weight</code> says how
      much. With no lockfile at all the axis is unassessed — not 0, and not
      100.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>"No lockfile" names the lock that could have been shipped.</strong>{" "}
      npm never publishes a package's <code>package-lock.json</code>, so for an
      npm package the lock it could carry is <code>npm-shrinkwrap.json</code>;
      a PyPI source distribution can carry its own, and a lock found in one is
      read. The gap is the publisher's. A server whose manifests declare no
      runtime dependency at all has nothing for this axis to measure: it is
      left unscored, as our limit, rather than credited with a 100, and when
      such a package runs an install hook the reason says so, because what
      the hook fetches is outside the source that was read. That is claimed
      only when the manifests establish it — a <code>setup.py</code>, a
      dynamic dependency list, a workspace or a nested project leaves the
      ordinary reason in place.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>A nested project's lockfile is not the server's.</strong> When
      the source is itself a project and a directory below it carries its own
      package manifest — a bundled template, a vendored research tree — that
      directory's lockfile pins that project. It is not scored, and it counts
      as part of the axis that was not measured, our gap rather than the
      publisher's: the tree cannot say whether such a lockfile also covers the
      server. The same rule already applies to licence and changelog files.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="paper doc-paper" aria-labelledby="table-heading">
    <h2 id="table-heading">Deductions</h2>
    <p>
      The axis opens at 100 and floors at 0. Each vulnerability deducts by
      its CVSS band and by whether the dependency is direct or transitive.
    </p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table class="ledger">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Severity</th>
            <th scope="col">CVSS</th>
            <th scope="col" class="num">Direct</th>
            <th scope="col" class="num">Transitive</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {rows.map((r, i) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">{cap(r.severity)}</th>
              <td>{cvss(i)}</td>
              <td class="num deduct">−{r.direct}</td>
              <td class="num deduct">−{r.transitive}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Direct dependencies cost more because the maintainer controls them and
      can update them, and because a transitive vulnerability more often sits
      in code a given server never reaches. Findings of one severity stack as
      they do on Code Safety — {stackingPercents.join("%, ")}%, then{" "}
      {stackingTailPercent}% — with the same rounding: only the axis score,
      half up.
    </p>
    <h3>Counting</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>One advisory is one finding. osv-scanner lists each alias of an advisory separately; they are grouped, never counted twice.</li>
      <li>A vulnerability with no CVSS score is listed on the page and not scored: the methodology defines no band for it.</li>
      <li>When directness cannot be told — a manifest we cannot parse — that is recorded, not guessed.</li>
      <li>“Fixed in” names the fix on the installed version's own release line.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="doc-section" aria-labelledby="limits-heading">
    <h2 id="limits-heading">Limits</h2>
    <p>
      There is no reachability analysis: a vulnerable function is counted
      whether or not the server calls it. That over-counts, most of all for
      transitive dependencies, which is part of why they deduct less.
    </p>
  </section>
</Doc>
